# Ajisai showcase — programs that say what only exact arithmetic can.
#
# Each entry is a short program and the stack it leaves, next to what the same
# computation does in binary floating point (IEEE 754 double). Nothing here is
# a trick of display: every `#=` line is a program and the exact stack it
# leaves, run by rust/tests/showcase_laws.rs against this file, and every
# `#:contract` line is verified by `ajisai check` before anything runs.
#
# Run the whole file once to define its Words (in the Playground, paste it into
# the Input surface and Run; with the CLI, put it in front of your program),
# then try the `#=` programs.

# ── Numbers are what they say ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A decimal literal is the rational it spells, so the classic float surprises
# do not happen. A double answers false, then 0, then 0 and 1.

#= 0.1 0.2 ADD 0.3 EQ → TRUE
#= 1e20 1 ADD 1e20 SUB → 1/1

# A sum is one value in every order — reordering or regrouping a FOLD never
# changes its answer, so a sum can be split, reversed or computed in parallel.
# The same three numbers summed in two orders give 0 and 1 in doubles.
#= [ 1e20 1 -1e20 ] 0 [ ADD ] FOLD → 1/1
#= [ 1e20 -1e20 1 ] 0 [ ADD ] FOLD → 1/1

# ── Irrationals are exact ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SQRT keeps its result as an exact algebraic number, so an identity of the
# golden ratio holds as equality, not as closeness.
#= 5 SQRT 1 ADD 2 DIV 'P' BIND P P MUL P 1 ADD EQ → TRUE

# n FIB — the n-th Fibonacci number by Binet's closed form, through the
# irrationals φ = (1+√5)/2 and ψ = (1−√5)/2. The radicals cancel exactly and
# the answer is an integer; in doubles the same formula is wrong from n = 71.
# Given a Vector of n it answers the whole sequence at once, with no loop.
#:contract FIB inputs=1 outputs=1 field=leaving
[ 'N' BIND 5 SQRT 'R' BIND 1 R ADD 2 DIV N POW 1 R SUB 2 DIV N POW SUB R DIV ] 'FIB' DEF
#= 100 FIB → 354224848179261915075/1
#= 1 12 RANGE FIB → [ 1/1 1/1 2/1 3/1 5/1 8/1 13/1 21/1 34/1 55/1 89/1 144/1 ]

# Powers of 1+√2 stay in normal form a + b√2, and a/b is the best rational
# approximation of √2 of its size (a Pell convergent); the error of that
# approximation is itself an exact number.
#= 1 2 SQRT ADD 10 POW → 3363/1+2378/1*sqrt(2)
#= 3363/2378 2 SQRT SUB → 3363/2378-sqrt(2)

# Rounding happens only where a program names it, and only into text: FORMAT
# settles every digit by exact comparison, the last one included.
#= 2 SQRT 50 FORMAT → '1.41421356237309504880168872420969807856967187537695'

# ── Division never stops ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Dividing by zero answers a point over zero, which is a number: in a Vector
# only the element divided by zero is affected, and the others are exact.
# A double gives Infinity here too, but 0/0 is NaN, which equals nothing,
# not even itself; 0/0 EQ 0/0 is TRUE in Ajisai.
#= [ 10 20 30 ] [ 2 0 5 ] DIV → [ 5/1 1/0 6/1 ]
#= 0 0 DIV 0 0 DIV EQ → TRUE

# A point over zero can be found and replaced element by element. EQ compares
# two whole values, so MAP asks it of each element.
#= [ 10 20 30 ] [ 2 0 5 ] DIV 'Q' BIND 0 Q Q [ 1/0 EQ ] MAP SELECT → [ 5/1 0/1 6/1 ]

# v MEAN — the arithmetic mean. The contract says before anything runs that it
# can leave the field: the divisor is computed, and the mean of nothing is
# 0/0. Declaring it `field=closed` instead is refused by `ajisai check`, which
# names the DIV it leaves at (rust/tests/showcase_laws.rs holds that refusal).
#:contract MEAN inputs=1 outputs=1 field=leaving
[ 'V' BIND V 0 [ ADD ] FOLD V LENGTH DIV ] 'MEAN' DEF
#= [ 1 2 3 4 ] MEAN → 5/2
#= [ ] MEAN → 0/0

# x HALF — dividing by a non-zero literal cannot leave the field, and the
# check proves it.
#:contract HALF inputs=1 outputs=1 field=closed
[ 2 DIV ] 'HALF' DEF
#= [ 1 2 3 ] HALF → [ 1/2 1/1 3/2 ]

# ── Truth is three-valued, as in SQL ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# A missing reading compares as UNKNOWN, the NIL it is, and FILTER keeps only
# what is TRUE, as WHERE does. Asking a point over zero for its order is
# UNKNOWN too: 1/0 has no sign, so it lies neither below nor above 5.
#= [ 3 NIL 5 ] 4 GT → [ FALSE NIL TRUE ]
#= [ 3 NIL 5 ] [ 4 GT ] FILTER → [ 5/1 ]
#= 1/0 5 LT NIL? → TRUE
